# Chapter Three — Don't Improve It

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. Written against the working crime engine, which remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 5 — The main course

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Lydia, Edwin, Marian, Robert, Nell, Paul.

= Immediate pressure: The table has discovered that it can enjoy itself. Lydia must resist using that goodwill to finish the business for which she assembled them.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

The waiter clears the soup. Edwin rises to help him.

LYDIA
Sit down.

EDWIN
In a moment.

He carries four bowls through the kitchen door and returns with four plates. The waiter follows with four more. On each is a white paper parcel folded into a long oval and tied with string.

ROBERT
What is it?

EDWIN
Sole.

ROBERT
Inside the paper.

EDWIN
Still sole.

Edwin cuts Lydia's string, opens the fold, and releases lemon-scented steam. He hands the scissors to Marian.

Marian opens hers neatly. Nell breaks the string with her fingers. Paul pulls at one end until the knot becomes smaller.

RUTH
Here.

PAUL
I can do it.

He pulls again.

Henry offers his pocketknife. Paul takes it.

PAUL
Thank you.

He cuts the string. Robert has torn his parcel open from the side. A gust of steam rises into his face.

MARIAN
Robert.

Marian pulls his plate away as Lydia reaches across with her napkin. They do it together, quickly and without collision.

Robert blinks.

ROBERT
I'm all right.

MARIAN
Wait before you lean over it.

LYDIA
Use this.

She gives him the napkin. Nell laughs. Paul does too.

ROBERT
I opened it.

MARIAN
You certainly did.

Paul closes the knife and returns it to Henry. Marian passes the dining scissors to Robert. They continue around the table until Edwin places them on the service cart.

Edwin sits at last.

For a while, they eat. There is enough butter in the fish and too much salt in the potatoes. Robert eats both without complaint.

Paul moves three capers to the edge of his paper. Ruth reaches toward his plate with her fork, catches herself, and returns to her own fish.

PAUL
I can remove them.

RUTH
I saw.

NELL
He ate them when he lived with me.

PAUL
I had somewhere to put them.

Nell holds out her plate. Paul transfers the capers to it.

MARIAN
You like capers?

NELL
No.

PAUL
This was the arrangement.

NELL
You had several.

PAUL
That one lasted.

Nell eats one of the capers anyway.

Paul turns to Henry.

PAUL
What do you do?

HENRY
Private investigations.

PAUL
People?

HENRY
Mostly husbands.

ROBERT
Do you find them?

HENRY
They tend to be where they said they weren't.

Robert nods as if this confirms something modest about the world.

PAUL
Is that how you know Ruth?

RUTH
No husband.

PAUL
I knew that part.

RUTH
Then you know enough.

Henry returns to his fish. Paul waits, then lets him.

NELL
Did you pack the green blanket?

LYDIA
No.

NELL
Good.

LYDIA
I gave it away.

NELL
To whom?

LYDIA
A woman on the fourth floor.

NELL
Which woman?

LYDIA
You don't know her.

NELL
Even better.

PAUL
Was there something wrong with it?

NELL
It follows you home.

MARIAN
It was a good blanket.

NELL
That was the problem. Lydia gave it to my mother when I was born. My mother gave it back when I left for school. Lydia gave it to me when Paul and I moved in together.

PAUL
I liked it.

NELL
When Paul left, I returned it before Lydia could give it to me again.

LYDIA
It was wool.

NELL
It was an assignment.

LYDIA
It kept you warm.

NELL
Those things can both be true.

Marian looks at Lydia. Lydia recognizes her own earlier sentence and gives Nell a small, unwilling smile.

LYDIA
The woman on the fourth floor has six children.

NELL
It will have plenty to do.

The room is quiet for a few bites.

PAUL
The first time I had dinner with all of you, June burned the chicken.

MARIAN
It wasn't burned.

ROBERT
It was black.

LYDIA
Only outside.

ROBERT
That is the part we saw.

NELL
You ate two pieces.

ROBERT
I was a guest.

PAUL
We opened every window. Marian put the chicken on the table. Lydia took it off. June put it back.

He looks at Ruth.

RUTH
I wasn't there.

PAUL
I've told you.

RUTH
That is not the same thing.

He waits.

Ruth continues eating.

PAUL
What did she say?

RUTH
Eat the middle.

Paul points at her with his fork.

PAUL
Exactly.

NELL
You still do that.

PAUL
Do what?

NELL
Stop before the sentence and wait for Ruth.

PAUL
She tells it better.

RUTH
That isn't what you ask me for.

Paul puts down his fork.

PAUL
No.

Nell looks from one of them to the other.

NELL
My mother said the middle was still chicken.

MARIAN
She said both.

LYDIA
She said, “The middle is still chicken. Eat the middle.”

ROBERT
That sounds right.

MARIAN
You were in the yard with the pan.

ROBERT
I came back.

EDWIN
Why was the pan in the yard?

ROBERT
It was smoking.

NELL
So was she.

Marian smiles.

MARIAN
She was furious with us for laughing.

NELL
She was laughing too.

MARIAN
Later.

NELL
At the table.

Marian thinks about it.

LYDIA
Nell is right. June laughed at the table. Then she told us not to improve it just because it was over.

Ruth's knife touches her plate.

The sound is slight. Paul hears it. Lydia does too.

RUTH
What did she say?

LYDIA
Don't improve it just because it's over.

Ruth looks at Paul. He folds a corner of his napkin inward.

HENRY
You've heard it.

RUTH
Something like it.

The waiter comes for their plates. Chairs move back to let him pass. The moment does not disappear, but the room goes on without it.

Paul takes a cream envelope from his inside pocket. He lays it beside his plate, looks at Nell, and puts his hand over it.

Nell reads her name upside down.

NELL
Is that for me?

PAUL
Yes.

NELL
Then give it to me.

PAUL
After dinner.

NELL
We're sitting here now.

PAUL
I know.

NELL
Do you want me to read it or hear it?

Paul considers this longer than he should.

PAUL
Hear it.

NELL
Then put the letter away.

He does.

NELL
After dinner. Alone. You begin.

PAUL
All right.

NELL
Don't say all right if you mean later.

PAUL
After dinner.

Nell nods once.

Edwin brings in a long apple tart. The waiter follows with coffee cups and leaves them empty.

MARIAN
Not enough cinnamon.

LYDIA
You haven't tasted it.

MARIAN
I can see.

Lydia reaches into her handbag and places a small silver tin between them.

Marian looks at it, then laughs. Lydia opens the tin. Marian dusts her piece and passes it to Robert, who uses too much.

The tin travels around the table.

Paul waits until Edwin is beside him.

PAUL
I left a carton with Mr. Bell downstairs.

EDWIN
He told me.

PAUL
May I collect it before the motor court closes?

EDWIN
After dessert.

He takes a brass key from his jacket and places it beside Paul's fork.

EDWIN
The service lift. Bring it back when you're done.

PAUL
Thank you.

Edwin continues around the table.

When the cinnamon reaches Henry, he takes a little.

LYDIA
You don't have to agree with us.

HENRY
I like cinnamon.

LYDIA
That is how it begins.

Henry passes the tin to Ruth. Paul nudges it toward her when she does not notice.

PAUL
You're with them now.

RUTH
I came with him.

HENRY
That is how it begins.

Ruth looks at Henry. She laughs.

Robert takes another bite of tart.

ROBERT
There was more cinnamon in the first one.

EDWIN
There was not.

ROBERT
Then I am getting used to it.

They keep eating.
